    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d751">

                <pb facs="0997" n="883" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg883"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.34/213: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Morgan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rio de Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-12-01T11:00:00-02:00">December 1, 1931—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 1—10:05 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>97. Brazilian Foreign Office has sent identical telegraphic instructions to
                    Ambassadors in Washington and London to inquire from the American and British
                    Governments whether one or the other is in a position to sell to the Brazilian
                    Government two light fast cruisers which can be used for protecting national
                    interests at home and for training seamen on board battleships <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Minos Geraes</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic">São
                        Paulo</hi> which it is the intention to scrap. Brazil is not in a position
                    to enter into a regional disarmament agreement with Argentina and Chile unless
                    she acquires cruisers in substitution of the ships on which their crews can
                    continue training.</p>
                <pb facs="0998" n="884" xml:id="pg884"/>
                <p>Shall be glad to learn the reply which the Department may eventually give the
                    Brazilian Ambassador.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Morgan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
